---
export const prerender = true;

import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const howItWorksSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "TechArticle",
  "headline": "How the NetSpeed Network Telemetry Engine Works",
  "description": "Technical guide explaining Anycast BGP server routing, concurrent Web Worker streams, loaded latency, bufferbloat detection, and quality scoring.",
  "author": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "NetSpeed"
  }
};
---

<Layout
  title="How It Works — NetSpeed Testing Methodology"
  description="Learn how NetSpeed measures your network performance. A technical overview of server selection, parallel streams, loaded latency, and quality scoring."
>
  <script
    type="application/ld+json"
    is:inline
    set:html={JSON.stringify(howItWorksSchema)}
  />
  <main class="flex-1 py-16 px-4 md:px-8">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto flex flex-col gap-12">
      {/* Header */}
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-4 border-b border-hairline pb-8">
        <span
          class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-mute block"
        >
          Methodology
        </span>
        <h1
          class="text-4xl md:text-6xl font-semibold tracking-tighter text-ink font-sans leading-none"
        >
          How the testing engine works.
        </h1>
        <p
          class="text-base md:text-lg text-body max-w-3xl leading-relaxed mt-2"
        >
          A detailed look at every stage of a NetSpeed test — from the moment
          you click Start to the final quality score. Each step is designed for
          accuracy, transparency, and real-world relevance.
        </p>
      </div>

      {/* Step 1: Server Selection */}
      <section class="flex flex-col gap-4 border-t border-hairline pt-12">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <span
            class="font-mono text-xs bg-primary text-on-primary w-7 h-7 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center shrink-0"
          >
            1
          </span>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink font-sans">
            Server selection via Anycast BGP
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed max-w-3xl">
          When you start a test, your browser sends a request to Cloudflare's
          network. Cloudflare uses Anycast BGP routing to direct that request to
          the nearest edge data center based on real-time network topology — not
          just geographic distance. This means you are connected to the server
          that offers the best network path, which is more accurate than
          connecting to the geographically closest server that might have poor
          routing.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed max-w-3xl">
          Cloudflare operates hundreds of data centers worldwide, so in most
          locations the nearest edge is within a few hundred kilometers. The
          engine displays which edge it connected to and the estimated distance,
          giving you full visibility into where your measurements are taken.
        </p>
      </section>

      {/* Step 2: Download Test */}
      <section class="flex flex-col gap-4 border-t border-hairline pt-12">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <span
            class="font-mono text-xs bg-primary text-on-primary w-7 h-7 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center shrink-0"
          >
            2
          </span>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink font-sans">
            Download test with parallel streams
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed max-w-3xl">
          The download test opens six parallel streams, each running in its own
          Web Worker. Web Workers are background threads that operate outside the
          main page thread, allowing the test to manage multiple connections
          simultaneously without affecting the user interface.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed max-w-3xl">
          Each stream downloads randomly generated binary data that cannot be
          cached by your ISP or compressed by intermediate networks. This forces
          your ISP to handle real, unoptimized data transit. The streams are
          adaptive — the engine monitors performance and adjusts the number of
          active connections based on what the network can handle. Once all
          streams complete, the total downloaded bytes and elapsed time produce
          your download speed.
        </p>
      </section>

      {/* Step 3: Upload Test */}
      <section class="flex flex-col gap-4 border-t border-hairline pt-12">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <span
            class="font-mono text-xs bg-primary text-on-primary w-7 h-7 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center shrink-0"
          >
            3
          </span>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink font-sans">
            Upload test with adaptive streams
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed max-w-3xl">
          The upload test works in reverse. Your browser pushes randomly
          generated binary data to the Cloudflare edge through multiple parallel
          streams. Upload testing is more challenging than download testing
          because browsers and operating systems typically prioritize download
          traffic, and many residential internet plans have much lower upload
          bandwidth.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed max-w-3xl">
          The engine calibrates the upload streams based on observed performance
          and adapts in real time. The result reflects the sustained upload
          capacity of your connection, not a momentary burst.
        </p>
      </section>

      {/* Step 4: Latency Measurement */}
      <section class="flex flex-col gap-4 border-t border-hairline pt-12">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <span
            class="font-mono text-xs bg-primary text-on-primary w-7 h-7 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center shrink-0"
          >
            4
          </span>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink font-sans">
            Latency and bufferbloat measurement
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed max-w-3xl">
          Latency is measured in two phases. First, the engine sends ping
          packets while the network is idle to measure unloaded latency — the
          baseline responsiveness of your connection. Then, during both the
          download and upload phases, it sends ping packets while the network is
          under heavy load to measure loaded latency.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed max-w-3xl">
          The difference between unloaded and loaded latency reveals
          bufferbloat — a condition where oversized buffers in your router or ISP
          equipment cause packets to queue up instead of being processed
          promptly. A connection with 15 ms unloaded latency that jumps to 300 ms
          under load has significant bufferbloat, which will cause lag spikes
          during gaming, choppy video calls, and general sluggishness whenever
          the network is busy.
        </p>
      </section>

      {/* Step 5: Jitter and Packet Loss */}
      <section class="flex flex-col gap-4 border-t border-hairline pt-12">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <span
            class="font-mono text-xs bg-primary text-on-primary w-7 h-7 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center shrink-0"
          >
            5
          </span>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink font-sans">
            Jitter and packet loss
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed max-w-3xl">
          Jitter is calculated by analyzing the variance in latency across
          multiple ping samples during the test. Consistent ping times indicate a
          stable connection. Wide fluctuations indicate instability, which causes
          audio dropouts on calls, stuttering in games, and inconsistent
          streaming quality.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed max-w-3xl">
          Packet loss is measured by tracking how many ping packets fail to reach
          the server and return. Even small amounts of packet loss — 1-2% — can
          noticeably degrade real-time applications. High packet loss usually
          indicates a problem with your physical connection, router, or ISP
          infrastructure.
        </p>
      </section>

      {/* Step 6: Quality Score */}
      <section class="flex flex-col gap-4 border-t border-hairline pt-12">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <span
            class="font-mono text-xs bg-primary text-on-primary w-7 h-7 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center shrink-0"
          >
            6
          </span>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink font-sans">
            Quality score
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed max-w-3xl">
          All metrics are combined into a single quality score that reflects how
          the connection actually feels. The score weighs not just raw speed but
          also latency, jitter, packet loss, and loaded latency. A connection
          with fast speeds but terrible bufferbloat will score lower than a
          connection with moderate speeds and excellent stability.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed max-w-3xl">
          The goal is to give you a single number that captures the overall
          quality of your connection for everyday use — streaming, gaming, video
          calls, browsing, and remote work. Every metric is also displayed
          individually so you can see exactly where your connection excels and
          where it falls short.
        </p>
      </section>

      {/* CTA */}
      <section class="border-t border-hairline pt-12">
        <div
          class="flex flex-col md:flex-row items-start md:items-center justify-between gap-6 bg-canvas-soft-2 p-8 rounded-lg border border-hairline"
        >
          <div class="flex flex-col gap-2 max-w-xl">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
              Try it yourself.
            </h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
              Run a complete test to see every metric in action. The results
              dashboard shows download, upload, latency, jitter, packet loss,
              loaded latency, and your overall quality score.
            </p>
          </div>
          <a
            href="/"
            class="bg-primary text-on-primary font-medium text-xs rounded-full py-2 px-6 hover:opacity-90 active:scale-95 transition-[opacity,transform] text-center select-none cursor-pointer inline-block whitespace-nowrap shadow-xs"
          >
            Run Speed Test
          </a>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>
